│ Mode: Agent                                                                                        │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                                     │
│ Max loops: 80                                                                                      │
│ Task: Take the findings already gathered in this workspace, validate each one against the live     │
│ target, then look for chains where one finding unlocks or amplifies another. A finding that has    │
│ not been demonstrated is a hypothesis, not a result.                                               │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Inventory what is already known                                                             │
│ Objective: Build a single list of everything previous passes discovered. Do not re-run             │
│ reconnaissance - the work is already done and repeating it wastes the run.                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. START FROM THE CONSOLIDATED NOTES. A consolidation pass ran immediately before this one and   │
│ wrote every finding gathered so far to notes, including material recovered from phase logs that    │
│ exists nowhere else. Read those notes FIRST and treat them as the authoritative input - they are   │
│ the complete record, and anything you do not carry out of this phase is lost for the rest of the   │
│ assessment.                                                                                        │
│   2. Count the consolidated entries and write the number down. You will be required to account for │
│ every one of them at the end of this playbook.                                                     │
│   3. Read the saved notes and any findings recorded in this workspace, and list the artifacts      │
│ already on disk (retrieved binaries, decompiled source, captured responses).                       │
│   4. Read the decompiled source and captured output you already have rather than fetching anything │
│ again.                                                                                             │
│   5. Write the inventory as two explicit lists: (1) CAPABILITIES - the keys, algorithms,           │
│ credentials and sessions you hold; (2) MATERIAL - the opaque values the target has actually        │
│ emitted (encrypted or encoded field values, blobs, cookies, tokens) together with the request that │
│ produced each one. Count both lists. If one of them is empty that is your next task, not a dead    │
│ end: a capability with nothing to apply it to is an unfinished finding, and so is material you     │
│ cannot yet read.                                                                                   │
│   6. STOP RULE: if roughly 8-10 consecutive steps on one lead produce no NEW evidence - no new     │
│ access, no new data, no change in the error you get back - abandon it, record what you tried and   │
│ what it would take to confirm, and move to the next lead. Returning later with fresh information   │
│ is fine; grinding is not.                                                                          │
│   7. COUNT REPETITION BY WHAT THE COMMAND DOES, NOT HOW IT IS SPELLED. Re-running the same tool    │
│ against the same file or endpoint with a widened context window, a reordered pattern or an extra   │
│ alternation is the SAME step, not a new one. Three attempts of that kind without new information   │
│ means the information is not there: the identifier you are searching for does not exist in that    │
│ file, or does not exist under that name. Change target or change approach - search a different     │
│ file, list what the file actually defines, or ask the running service - rather than re-phrasing    │
│ the same query a fourth time.                                                                      │
│   8. Before repeating any search, say what NEW thing the next attempt could reveal that the last   │
│ one could not. If there is no answer, that is the signal to stop.                                  │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Validate every finding against the live target                                              │
│ Objective: Turn hypotheses into demonstrated facts. An unvalidated secret is worth reporting only  │
│ as a possibility.                                                                                  │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. For each piece of recovered MATERIAL, design a concrete test that proves whether it is live   │
│ and accepted by the target. If the material is used by code you have decompiled, reimplement that  │
│ code exactly - same algorithm, mode, padding, encoding and any envelope or timestamp structure.    │
│ The recovered source is the specification; do not guess at the format.                             │
│   2. MEASURE THE MATERIAL, DO NOT EYEBALL IT. Extraction routinely corrupts a secret by a byte or  │
│ two - a trailing newline, quote, or shell escaping - and a value that is right except for its      │
│ length behaves exactly like a wrong one. Strip surrounding whitespace and quotes, then run code    │
│ that prints the length of the exact value you are about to use and quote that output. Never write  │
│ a byte count in prose: counting repeated padding characters by eye has produced a wrong figure     │
│ more than once, and the machine does not miscount. A PLAUSIBLE LENGTH IS NOT A CORRECT ONE - most  │
│ block ciphers accept several key sizes, so a wrong extraction often lands on a legal size and      │
│ simply produces garbage. 'This is a valid key length' is not confirmation.                         │
│   3. USE THE COPY YOU MEASURED. Read the literal from the source into a variable, print its length │
│ from that variable, and pass THAT SAME variable to the cipher - retyping the value into a second   │
│ command is where a character is gained or lost, and the copy you measured is then not the copy you │
│ used. Prefer the source literal verbatim over reconstructing it through hex or base64; if you must │
│ convert, decode back and compare character for character. When you record it for later phases,     │
│ record the measured length and the exact literal together and say how you measured it - a later    │
│ phase will trust what you write and will not re-derive it.                                         │
│   4. SELF-TEST YOUR REIMPLEMENTATION BEFORE TRUSTING ANY RESULT. An incorrect implementation and   │
│ correctly-rejected material produce the same rejection, so a failed request tells you nothing      │
│ until you have proven your own code. Do both checks:                                               │
│   (i) ROUND TRIP - transform a known value forward then back and confirm you recover the original  │
│ exactly. This proves internal consistency, and nothing more.                                       │
│   (ii) AGAINST THE TARGET'S OWN OUTPUT - take any value the TARGET produced using this material (a │
│ token, cookie, stored field, or a captured response) and process it with your implementation.      │
│ Recovering sensible plaintext from something the server generated is the only proof your           │
│ implementation matches theirs.                                                                     │
│   5. Sanity-check the SHAPE of what you produce against a real sample from the target: length,     │
│ block alignment, whether a prefix such as an IV or nonce is present, and the encoding. A           │
│ structurally plausible output that still fails is a strong hint the key bytes or their derivation  │
│ are wrong, not the framing.                                                                        │
│   6. TREAT REJECTIONS AS AN ORACLE, AND AS EVIDENCE ABOUT YOUR REQUEST AT LEAST AS OFTEN AS ABOUT  │
│ YOUR MATERIAL. Distinguish the failure stages: rejection during decryption or verification (your   │
│ material or algorithm is wrong), acceptance followed by a parse or schema complaint (the crypto is │
│ RIGHT and your inner structure is wrong), and complaints about an unknown or unsupported operation │
│ (both are right and you named something the server does not offer). Moving from one class of error │
│ to another is progress. This matters most when the material came from the application's own stored │
│ data: such a value is very unlikely to be wrong, so a rejection points at the request shape first. │
│ Vary one thing at a time.                                                                          │
│   7. ENUMERATE THE ACCEPTED VALUES FROM THE SOURCE FIRST - required, not a precaution. Before      │
│ composing any request to a dispatcher, router, command or batch style endpoint, read the           │
│ decompiled handler for what it actually accepts: switch/case labels, if-else comparisons, enum     │
│ members, dictionary keys or its route table. Write that list down before sending anything. NEVER   │
│ invent an operation name - a placeholder such as 'Test' or a guessed resource is rejected as       │
│ unknown even when your credentials and encryption are perfect, which wastes the attempt and looks  │
│ like the material failed when it did not. Work the enumerated values in order of what returns the  │
│ most data or touches the most sensitive handler; a value rejected as unknown while others are      │
│ accepted tells you the dispatcher works and your envelope is valid.                                │
│   8. ANSWER 'WHAT DOES THIS REQUEST NEED' BY SEARCHING BEFORE PROBING. Guessing a request's shape  │
│ and reading the rejection is the slowest way to learn it and the easiest to misread, and earlier   │
│ phases have usually already collected the answer. Before composing a request to any endpoint you   │
│ have not successfully used, search in this order: saved notes and prior phase reports describing   │
│ it; any machine-readable API description the target publishes (OpenAPI or Swagger documents, WSDL, │
│ GraphQL introspection, metadata or discovery endpoints); the decompiled source for the handler,    │
│ its route attribute and its request type; and the client-side code for the call site. This applies │
│ to authentication requests especially, where it is most often skipped - such endpoints commonly    │
│ need more than a username and password, such as a provider or scheme name, a realm, a client       │
│ identifier, a CSRF or antiforgery token, or a specific content type.                               │
│   9. Say where the shape came from and how sure you are of it. A field list read from a request    │
│ type in source, or from a published specification, is reliable. A field list inferred from a URL   │
│ or a guess is not, and a request built from one that fails has not tested anything. Record what    │
│ you found so a later phase does not repeat the search.                                             │
│   10. OBSERVE THE APPLICATION DOING IT ITSELF. If a browser tool is available and the application  │
│ has a user interface for the operation, drive that interface and capture the request it issues -   │
│ route, method, headers, content type, every field name and its exact casing - then mirror it. A    │
│ working example produced by the application is the most reliable specification obtainable, it      │
│ costs one page load, and it settles questions that reading source can leave open.                  │
│   11. ESTABLISH BOTH POLES BEFORE JUDGING A CREDENTIAL. A rejection says the request was not       │
│ accepted; it does not say whether the credential, the field set, the route or the encoding was     │
│ wrong, and without a reference for acceptance the only reading available is 'the material is bad'  │
│ - which discards correct work. Get references first: read the route from source rather than        │
│ guessing paths, since a guessed path returns the same rejection as a wrong credential; if any      │
│ credentials are already known, authenticate with them once and keep the success response - status, │
│ headers, cookies, body; and send a deliberately wrong credential to the same route with the same   │
│ shape and keep that response too.                                                                  │
│   12. With both references, a rejection is interpretable. If your attempt does not even match the  │
│ FAILURE shape - a different status, a different error, a complaint about a missing field - then    │
│ your request is malformed and the credential has not been tested yet. Only a response matching the │
│ failure shape exactly is evidence about the credential itself. State which of the two you          │
│ observed. Take the credential from where it was produced rather than retyping it: if a script      │
│ decrypted it, have that script write it to a file and read the file in the request.                │
│   13. Prefer a safe read-only proof first (something that returns data or is accepted) over        │
│ anything that writes or changes state.                                                             │
│   14. RECORD THE EVIDENCE THE MOMENT IT IS TRUE. As soon as something is shown to work - a value   │
│ decrypts, a token is accepted, an endpoint returns what it should not - write the exact request    │
│ and the exact response with the notes tool immediately, in that same step, before moving to the    │
│ next lead. Do not save it up to write later. This is not bookkeeping: this phase can be cut off at │
│ any point when the step budget runs out, and anything not written down when that happens is lost   │
│ for the rest of the assessment no matter how conclusively you proved it.                           │
│   15. If validation fails, do not discard the finding immediately: check your reimplementation     │
│ against the source, and distinguish 'my test was wrong' from 'the material is not accepted'. Say   │
│ which one you concluded and why.                                                                   │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Chain findings into higher severity                                                         │
│ Objective: Look for combinations. Findings that are individually moderate are frequently critical  │
│ together.                                                                                          │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Cross every item of MATERIAL against every item of SURFACE and ask: does this material        │
│ unlock, authenticate to, decrypt, sign for, or bypass a control on this surface?                   │
│   2. Look specifically for surfaces that were previously refused, errored, or returned less data   │
│ than expected - recovered material often turns exactly those into successes.                       │
│   3. Watch for dispatch, batch, proxy or import style endpoints that forward requests internally:  │
│ reaching a handler by that route can bypass the checks applied to its direct route. Compare what   │
│ the direct route returns against what the indirect route returns.                                  │
│   4. STOP AND JOIN THEM - whenever you hold BOTH a secret AND an opaque stored value (encrypted    │
│ token, protected field, cookie, unexplained blob), attempt the decode immediately, as the next     │
│ action, before writing either up. Two findings recorded separately read as 'a key leaked' and      │
│ 'some data leaked'; joined they are frequently FULL ACCOUNT TAKEOVER. Assume the join works and    │
│ disprove it.                                                                                       │
│   5. Try to decrypt or decode anything opaque you receive using the material you already           │
│ recovered; matching a value back to a known algorithm is frequently the whole finding. Do this for │
│ every account or object you hold a value for, not just the first.                                  │
│   6. A RESULT PROVEN AGAINST ONE OBJECT IS A RESULT OF UNKNOWN SIZE. The moment a technique works  │
│ on one account, record, file or record-holder, apply it to the others you can enumerate. Stopping  │
│ at the first success answers 'is this possible' and leaves 'how much of the system does this       │
│ reach' unanswered - and the second question is what determines severity and what a reader needs.   │
│   7. Cover the privileged cases deliberately. If the population includes accounts or objects with  │
│ more authority than the one you proved - administrators, operators, service or system accounts,    │
│ records belonging to other tenants - test those specifically rather than sampling whichever came   │
│ first. A technique that reaches an ordinary account and one that reaches an administrative account │
│ are different findings.                                                                            │
│   8. Report the count and the composition: how many of the known population were tested, how many  │
│ were affected, and which privileged ones were among them. If you could not enumerate the           │
│ population, say what you did test and that the true breadth is unmeasured - that is a real         │
│ limitation, not a detail to omit.                                                                  │
│   9. RECORD EACH LINK AS IT IS PROVEN, not once the whole chain is assembled. A chain that is      │
│ three quarters demonstrated is still a serious finding; a chain you never wrote down is nothing.   │
│ Write a note per proven step, and update it as the chain grows.                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Report chains with evidence and impact                                                      │
│ Objective: Produce a defensible write-up of what was demonstrated.                                 │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Report each chain as an ordered sequence of steps, each with the request made and the         │
│ response received.                                                                                 │
│   2. State impact concretely - what data or capability was obtained, and for how many objects or   │
│ accounts - rather than in the abstract.                                                            │
│   3. Establish impact by contrast: show the same result cannot be obtained without the recovered   │
│ material or the chained route.                                                                     │
│   4. Label every finding with its proof level and be strict about it: DEMONSTRATED (you executed   │
│ it and the response proves it - quote that response), INFERRED (follows from evidence but was not  │
│ executed), or UNVERIFIED (suspected from a version number, library name or code pattern only).     │
│   5. Identifying a framework, library or version is NOT a vulnerability finding. A known issue in  │
│ that component is UNVERIFIED until you execute it against this target and observe the effect. Do   │
│ not write 'confirmed' on the basis of what software is present, and do not cite an advisory        │
│ identifier unless you have checked it applies to this deployment.                                  │
│   6. For code execution specifically, the proof is OUTPUT of a command you chose, returned by the  │
│ target - not a payload you sent, and not an error that merely looks interesting. Match the payload │
│ to the actual platform; a Windows shell command against a Unix host proves nothing either way.     │
│   7. Set severity from what was DEMONSTRATED. An unproven critical theory is reported as a lead to │
│ investigate, never as a critical finding - overstating severity destroys the credibility of the    │
│ whole report.                                                                                      │
│   8. Name the specific root cause for each link in the chain (the assembly, type and member, or    │
│ the endpoint and parameter) so it is directly actionable.                                          │
│   9. CARRY EVERY CONSOLIDATED FINDING FORWARD - not only the ones that chained. Chaining is        │
│ additive: it creates new findings, it does not replace the inputs. A finding that did not combine  │
│ with anything is still a finding, and the next phase can only review what reaches it.              │
│   10. Go back to the count you recorded in the first phase and reconcile against it explicitly.    │
│ Every consolidated entry must end in exactly one of three states: folded into a chain (name the    │
│ chain), carried forward unchanged, or dropped as a proven duplicate (name the entry it             │
│ duplicates). There is no fourth state - if you cannot say which applies, it is carried forward.    │
│   11. Write the full carried-forward set to notes, one note per finding, preserving each finding's │
│ original evidence, proof level and severity. Do NOT summarise several findings into one note and   │
│ do not drop the low-severity and informational ones - missing headers, verbose errors and version  │
│ disclosure are exactly what falls out here, and they are both reportable and inputs to future      │
│ chains.                                                                                            │
│   12. Findings you could not validate are carried forward too, labelled UNVERIFIED with what you   │
│ tried. Failing to prove something is a result; silently discarding it is not.                      │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
